---
// src/pages/graphic-engine.astro
// Technical breakdown of the XEMA custom C++ engine.
// Edit the content sections below to add/update technical details.

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="XEMA Engine · Technical Breakdown — Fernando Fernández Andúeza"
  description="Technical architecture of XEMA — a custom C++ game engine with OpenGL 4.5, ECS, Wave Function Collapse, and Nintendo Switch port."
>
  <div class="container">

    <!-- ── PAGE HEADER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="page-header">
      <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to portfolio</a>

      <div style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:.875rem; margin-bottom:.875rem; flex-wrap:wrap;">
        <span class="badge badge-personal"><span class="badge-dot"></span>Personal project</span>
        <span class="card-engine">C++ &nbsp;·&nbsp; OpenGL 4.5 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Nintendo Switch</span>
      </div>

      <h1>XEMA — Custom 3D Engine</h1>
      <p class="page-intro">
        A high-performance C++ game engine built from scratch to master the full rendering pipeline,
        low-level memory architecture, and cross-platform deployment — including a Nintendo Switch port.
        Every system was written without relying on a pre-existing engine framework.
      </p>

      <div class="card-links" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
        <a href="https://github.com/YOUR_XEMA_REPO" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn-secondary">
          View source on GitHub ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── CORE SYSTEMS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2>Core systems</h2>
      <div class="tech-grid">

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Entity Component System</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Data-oriented design</strong> — components stored in contiguous arrays for cache efficiency.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>O(1) component access</strong> via ID-based entity management — no map lookups on the hot path.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Scene graph</strong> — parent-child hierarchy integrated directly into the component arrays.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Job System</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Thread pool</strong> using std::future for fire-and-forget async tasks.
            </li>
            <li>
              Handles <strong>asynchronous model loading</strong> and Wave Function Collapse generation
              without stalling the main loop.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Async I/O</strong> layer that keeps frame time stable during level streaming.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Procedural Generation</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Wave Function Collapse</strong> for tile-based level generation using KayKit tilesets.
            </li>
            <li>
              Custom <strong>entropy calculation</strong> — diagnosed and fixed a critical bug causing
              non-deterministic collapse order.
            </li>
            <li>
              WFC runs on a <strong>background job thread</strong> so generation never blocks the render loop.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── GRAPHICS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2>Graphics &amp; rendering pipeline</h2>
      <div class="tech-grid">

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>OpenGL 4.5 DSA Pipeline</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Direct State Access (DSA)</strong> — eliminates redundant bind calls for cleaner, faster state management.
            </li>
            <li>
              Custom vertex buffer and VAO management, instanced rendering, and UBO-based uniform blocks.
            </li>
            <li>
              Hand-written <strong>GLSL shaders</strong> for every rendering stage.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Rendering Techniques</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Deferred Shading</strong> — G-Buffer pass stores position, normal, albedo, and material data.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>PCF Shadow Mapping</strong> — Percentage Closer Filtering for smooth shadow edges.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>PBR — Metallic/Roughness</strong> workflow with Cook-Torrance BRDF.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Platform Abstraction (HAL)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Zero-overhead abstraction</strong> — platform differences resolved at link-time,
              no virtual dispatch on the render path.
            </li>
            <li>
              Single rendering interface that switches between OpenGL (PC) and EGL (Switch)
              without code changes in higher layers.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── NINTENDO SWITCH PORT ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2>Nintendo Switch port</h2>
      <div class="tech-grid">

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Platform target</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Ported via <strong>libnx</strong> (homebrew SDK) and <strong>EGL</strong>
              for the OpenGL context on Nintendo Switch hardware.
            </li>
            <li>
              Supports both <strong>Docked mode (1080p)</strong> and <strong>Handheld mode (720p)</strong>
              with automatic resolution switching.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Build system</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Premake5</strong> generates platform-specific build files from a single Lua script.
            </li>
            <li>
              Integrates <strong>devkitPro Makefile</strong> for the Switch target —
              one codebase, two platform outputs.
            </li>
            <li>
              No engine fork — the same C++ source compiles for both PC and Switch.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── ADDITIONAL SYSTEMS ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2>Additional systems</h2>
      <div class="tech-grid">

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Physics &amp; scripting</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>PhysX 5</strong> integration via a custom Physics Manager that owns
              the scene and dispatches simulation steps.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Lua scripting</strong> via sol2 — exposes engine types to Lua
              without manual binding boilerplate.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tech-card">
          <h3>Memory &amp; low-level</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Manual memory management throughout — no garbage collection, no hidden allocations.
            </li>
            <li>
              Custom allocators for component pools and asset caches to avoid heap fragmentation.
            </li>
            <li>
              Profiled and optimized hot paths using platform-specific tools.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="padding-bottom: 5rem;"></div>

  </div><!-- /.container -->

</Layout>
